{"annotationScore": 5.0, "comments": [{"commentType": "FUNCTION", "molecule": "Ubiquitin", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62987", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Exists either covalently attached to another protein, or free (unanchored). When covalently bound, it is conjugated to target proteins via an isopeptide bond either as a monomer (monoubiquitin), a polymer linked via different Lys residues of the ubiquitin (polyubiquitin chains) or a linear polymer linked via the initiator Met of the ubiquitin (linear polyubiquitin chains). Polyubiquitin chains, when attached to a target protein, have different functions depending on the Lys residue of the ubiquitin that is linked: Lys-6-linked may be involved in DNA repair; Lys-11-linked is involved in ERAD (endoplasmic reticulum-associated degradation) and in cell-cycle regulation; Lys-29-linked is involved in proteotoxic stress response and cell cycle; Lys-33-linked is involved in kinase modification; Lys-48-linked is involved in protein degradation via the proteasome; Lys-63-linked is involved in endocytosis, DNA-damage responses as well as in signaling processes leading to activation of the transcription factor NF-kappa-B. Linear polymer chains formed via attachment by the initiator Met lead to cell signaling. Ubiquitin is usually conjugated to Lys residues of target proteins, however, in rare cases, conjugation to Cys or Ser residues has been observed. When polyubiquitin is free (unanchored-polyubiquitin), it also has distinct roles, such as in activation of protein kinases, and in signaling"}]}, {"commentType": "FUNCTION", "molecule": "Large ribosomal subunit protein eL40", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62987", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Component of the 60S subunit of the ribosome. Ribosomal protein L40 is essential for translation of a subset of cellular transcripts, and especially for cap-dependent translation of vesicular stomatitis virus mRNAs"}]}, {"cofactors": [{"cofactorCrossReference": {"database": "ChEBI", "id": "CHEBI:29105"}, "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P0DXC2", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "name": "Zn(2+)"}], "commentType": "COFACTOR", "note": {"texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P0DXC2", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Binds 1 Zn(2+) per subunit."}]}}, {"commentType": "SUBUNIT", "molecule": "Large ribosomal subunit protein eL40", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62987", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Part of the 60S ribosomal subunit. Interacts with UBQLN1 (via UBA domain)"}]}, {"commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION", "molecule": "Ubiquitin", "subcellularLocations": [{"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250"}], "id": "SL-0086", "value": "Cytoplasm"}}, {"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250"}], "id": "SL-0191", "value": "Nucleus"}}]}, {"commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION", "molecule": "Large ribosomal subunit protein eL40", "subcellularLocations": [{"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62984", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "id": "SL-0086", "value": "Cytoplasm"}}]}, {"commentType": "PTM", "molecule": "Ubiquitin", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62987", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Phosphorylated at Ser-65 by PINK1 during mitophagy. Phosphorylated ubiquitin specifically binds and activates parkin (PRKN), triggering mitophagy. Phosphorylation does not affect E1-mediated E2 charging of ubiquitin but affects discharging of E2 enzymes to form polyubiquitin chains. It also affects deubiquitination by deubiquitinase enzymes such as USP30"}]}, {"commentType": "PTM", "molecule": "Ubiquitin", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62987", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Mono-ADP-ribosylated at the C-terminus by PARP9, a component of the PPAR9-DTX3L complex. ADP-ribosylation requires processing by E1 and E2 enzymes and prevents ubiquitin conjugation to substrates such as histones"}]}, {"commentType": "PTM", "molecule": "Large ribosomal subunit protein eL40", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250", "id": "P62987", "source": "UniProtKB"}], "value": "Trimethylation of Lys-98 ('Lys-22' of the mature chain) by SMYD5 promotes translation elongation and protein synthesis"}]}, {"commentType": "MISCELLANEOUS", "texts": [{"value": "Ubiquitin is encoded by 4 different genes. UBA52 and RPS27A genes code for a single copy of ubiquitin fused to the ribosomal proteins eL40 and eS31, respectively. UBB and UBC genes code for a polyubiquitin precursor with exact head to tail repeats, the number of repeats differ between species and strains"}]}, {"commentType": "MISCELLANEOUS", "texts": [{"value": "For a better understanding, features related to ubiquitin are only indicated for the first chain"}]}, {"commentType": "SIMILARITY", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "In the N-terminal section; belongs to the ubiquitin family"}]}, {"commentType": "SIMILARITY", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "In the C-terminal section; belongs to the eukaryotic ribosomal protein eL40 family"}]}], "entryAudit": {"entryVersion": 133, "firstPublicDate": "2004-08-31", "lastAnnotationUpdateDate": "2026-11-04", "lastSequenceUpdateDate": "2010-08-10", "sequenceVersion": 2}, "entryType": "UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)", "extraAttributes": {"countByCommentType": {"COFACTOR": 1, "FUNCTION": 2, "MISCELLANEOUS": 2, "PTM": 3, "SIMILARITY": 2, "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION": 2, "SUBUNIT": 1}, "countByFeatureType": {"Beta strand": 2, "Binding site": 4, "Chain": 2, "Cross-link": 8, "Domain": 1, "Helix": 2, "Modified residue": 3, "Site": 3, "Turn": 1}, "uniParcId": "UPI0000021092"}, "features": [{"description": "Ubiquitin", "featureId": "PRO_0000396436", "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 76}, "start": {"modif